1. e4 c5 {...In the first round of the premier tournament at Blackpool it was evident that the players were suffering slightly from lack of practice owing to the war and consequently the games were slightly marred by error. Nevertheless the play was highly interesting. The refugee master Mieses played the Sicilian defence against C. R. Gumhill and secured early a promising attack, which, however, did not seem to come to fruition. The game was adjourned in position' which appears to be likely to result in a draw,'there being bishops of opposite colour. H. G. Rhodes, of Preston, played pawn to king’s fourth on the fifth move of a queen’s gambit against Wahltuch. Wahltuch, playing exceedingly well, beat off the attack and stood if any­thing better than his opponent. Unfortunately, however, he allowed his clock to run out and lost on the time­limit. Normally the game should have been drawn. G. Abrahams played white against Hooper, the new correspondence champion of the British Chess Federa­tion and a well-known Surrey player. The game, which developed from a Catalan opening, was not free from error on-the part of both players and ended fittingly in a draw after a good struggle. In other, tournaments the following Manchester players are competing: Mr. Rappaport is playing in the major tournament and Mr. Henry Erin, captain of Manchester University chess team, is now playing In the first-class tournament. (Manchester Guardian, 18 Jul 1944)} 1/2-1/2

