1. e4 c5 2. b4 cxb4 3. Bb2 d5 4. exd5 Qxd5 5. c4 Qd8 6. Nf3 Nf6 7. d4 e6 8. Bd3 Bd7 9. O-O Bc6 10. d5 exd5 11. Re1+ Be7 12. Qe2 dxc4 13. Bxc4 Bxf3 14. gxf3 Kf8 15. Nd2 Nc6 16. Ne4 Nxe4 17. fxe4 Qe8 18. Kh1 Rd8 19. Bd5 Bf6 20. e5 Be7 21. Qf3 h5 22. Rg1 Rh6 23. e6 Bf6 24. Bxf6 Rxf6 25. Qxh5 Rh6 26. Qg5 Rg6 27. exf7 Qe5 28. Qxg6 Qxd5+ 29. Rg2 Qxf7 30. Qh7 Ne7 31. Qe4 Nd5 32. Rag1 Re8 33. Qd3 Nf4 34. Qd6+ Re7 35. Rxg7 Qd5+ 36. Qxd5 Nxd5 37. Rxe7 {Source: BCM, Sept 1944, p210.  [In] the second round of the premier tournament at Blackpool yesterday the play was rather more vigorous than in the first round, which is normal in chess contests. Against Mieses, Abrahams, playing white, adopted an original form of the wing gambit in answer to his opponent's Sicilian defence. He did not attempt to regain the gambit pawn but maintained a continuous attack in the centre and towards the King’s side, sacrificing another pawn in the process. The attack, which was well maintained and well resisted, eventually proved successful; and at the adjournment Abrahams had achieved a definite superiority of position, probably suffici­ent to produce a win. (Manchester Guardian, 19 July 1944)} 1-0

