1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 d5 4. Bg5 Be7 5. e3 O-O 6. Nf3 h6 7. Bh4 b6 8. cxd5 exd5 9. Be2 Be6 10. Ne5 Ne4 11. Bxe7 Qxe7 12. Rc1 Rd8 13. O-O c5 14. Bb5 a6 15. Bc6 Nxc3 16. Rxc3 Ra7 17. Ba4 Bf5 18. Bc2 Bxc2 19. Qxc2 c4 20. e4 f6 21. Ng6 Qxe4 22. Rxc4 Kh7 23. Nh4 Re8 24. Rc3 Nd7 25. f3 Qxc2 26. Rxc2 Nf8 27. Kf2 g6 28. g3 Rae7 29. Ng2 g5 30. Rd1 Ne6 31. Rdd2 Ng7 32. Rc6 Re6 33. Rxe6 Rxe6 34. Ne3 Rd6 35. Rc2 Kg6 36. g4 f5 37. Rc7 Ne8 38. Re7 fxg4 39. Rxe8 {Source: BCM, Sept 1944, p215  The- game between Rhodes and Hooper was postponed until evening by arrangement. In an adjourned game from the first round Mieses drew with Gumhill, as was anticipated yesterday. In other tournaments Manchester players are holding their own. These include Messrs. MacMillan, Turner, Watson. Mort, and Milner, the captain of the Manchester Chess Club. Young Rappaport lost yesterday to a very experienced London player, but should do well. (Manchester Guardian, 19 July 1944)  In the postponed game from the second round at the Blackpool chess tournament yesterday Hooper dis­tinguished himself by outplaying Rhodes with some subtle middle-game play and a general control of position. He eventually turned his positional advant­age into material advantage and won one of the most convincing games of the tournament. In the adjourned game from the second round Abrahams won against Mieses, as was anticipated. (Manchester Guardian, 20 July 1944)} 1-0

