1. d4 d5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. e3 Bf5 4. Bd3 e6 5. Bxf5 exf5 6. Qd3 Qc8 7. Nbd2 Be7 8. Nf1 O-O 9. Bd2 Ne4 10. c4 c6 11. cxd5 cxd5 12. Rc1 Nc6 13. a3 Qd7 14. Bc3 Rac8 15. N3d2 a6 16. Nxe4 fxe4 17. Qe2 f5 18. f4 Bh4+ 19. g3 Bf6 20. Rd1 Qf7 21. g4 Bh4+ 22. Kd2 h6 23. Ng3 g6 24. gxf5 gxf5 25. Qg2 Kh7 26. Qh3 Be7 27. Rhg1 b5 28. Rg2 a5 29. Rdg1 Qe6 30. Nxf5 Rf6 31. Rg7+ Kh8 32. Qg3 Rxf5 33. Rh7+ {Source: BCM, Sept 1944, p212  The game between Wahltuch and Gumhill was also a good contest. In a slow Queen’s gambit, Gumhill with black Equalised at an early stage, but Wahltuch, who is essentially a clear­sighted combative player, succeeded in opening the game up and resourcefully built up a promising attack which resulted in victory rather more easily than he expected. It is thought that Gumhill could have avoided defeat very near to the end. (Manchester Guardian, 19 July 1944)} 1-0

