1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 exd5 4. Nf3 Bd6 5. Bd3 Ne7 6. O-O O-O 7. Ng5 Bf5 8. Bxf5 Nxf5 9. Qh5 h6 10. Nf3 Qf6 11. Nc3 c6 12. Be3 Nd7 13. Rad1 Nxe3 14. fxe3 Qe6 15. Rde1 Nf6 16. Qh4 Bb4 17. Nd2 Rae8 18. Ncb1 Ne4 19. c3 Be7 20. Qh5 Bg5 21. Nf3 Qc8 22. Nxg5 hxg5 23. Na3 Re6 24. Nc2 Rh6 25. Qe2 Qc7 26. g3 f5 27. Rf3 g4 28. Rff1 Rff6 29. Nb4 Rh3 30. Rf2 Nxf2 31. Qxf2 Rfh6 32. Qf4 Qxf4 33. exf4 Rxh2 34. Re8+ Kh7 35. Nd3 Rh1+ 36. Kf2 R6h2+ 37. Ke3 Rg1 {Source: BCM, Sept 1944, p206  In the third round Hooper again played meritorious chess. His opponent was Gurnhill who, in an exchange varia­tion of the French Defence, made a slow opening slower by venturing into a pre­mature excursion from which he had to retreat. Hooper steadily increased his advantage, and although Gurnhill missed one or two possibilities in defence, succeeded in winning a quite convincing game. Hooper plays steady positional chess of an apparently patient type which is capable of clear analysis and quite subtle perception.} 0-1

