1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 e6 4. Nc3 dxc4 5. a4 Bb4 6. e3 b5 7. Bd2 a5 8. axb5 Bxc3 9. Bxc3 cxb5 10. b3 Bb7 11. bxc4 b4 12. Bb2 Nf6 13. Bd3 Ne4 14. Qc2 f5 15. O-O O-O 16. Ne5 Qg5 17. f3 Qxe3+ 18. Kh1 b3 19. Qxb3 Ng3+ 20. hxg3 Rf6 21. Ng4 fxg4 22. Bxh7+ Kxh7 23. Qxe3 gxf3 24. gxf3 Nd7 25. d5 Rh6+ 26. Kg2 exd5 27. cxd5 {Source: 'A History of Lancashire Chess 1871-2009' by Mike Conroy.  Against Rhodes Abrahams played his own variation of the Slav Defence (the Abrahams variation) and on the 17th move won a pawn, though at the cost of some positional difficulty. At this point he let himself be tempted by what appeared to be a pretty mating attack but his opponent was able to discover an unexpected counter and won in 27 moves. The present position of the players Is that Hooper leads with 2½ points, Wahltuch and Rhodes each have 2 points, Abrahams has 1½ points, and Mieses and Gurnhill ½ point each. In the Major tournament Dewing leads with 3 points out of a possible three. (Manchester Guardian, 20 July 1944)} 1-0

