1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e5 3. dxe5 Ng4 4. Bf4 Nc6 5. Nf3 Bb4+ 6. Nc3 Qe7 7. Qd5 f6 8. exf6 Nxf6 9. Qd3 Ne4 10. e3 d6 11. Nd2 Bf5 12. Ndxe4 Bxe4 13. Qd2 O-O 14. O-O-O Qf6 15. f3 Bg6 16. Bg3 Qxc3+ 17. bxc3 Ba3+ 18. Qb2 Bxb2+ 19. Kxb2 Rae8 20. e4 Ne5 21. c5 dxc5 22. Rd5 Nf7 23. Bxc7 b6 24. Bc4 h6 25. Rd7 Kh7 26. Rhd1 a5 27. Bd6 Ne5 28. Bxe5 Rxe5 29. Rb7 Rg5 30. Rd2 Rf6 31. Rb8 Re5 32. Bg8+ Kh8 33. Rdd8 {Source: BCM, Sept 1944, p211  Between Mieses and Hooper a Buda­pest Counter-gambit was played. Mieses pressed the counter-attack but was tan- able to achieve a decisive result. Instead of reconciling himself with drawing chances, he continued an attack much. Hooper was able to beat off and in the end gain a convincing win. ... These victories by Gurnhill and Abrahams have considerably affected the balance in the tournament. Rhodes and Wahltuch are now out of reach of the first prize: Hooper is sure of at least a share of the first and Abrahams has.a remote chance of joining it. The scores are Hooper 3½, Abrahams 2½, Wahltuch 2, Rhodes 2, Gurnhill 1½, Mieses ½. (Manchester Guardian, 21 July 1944)} 1-0

