1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 dxc4 5. e3 b5 6. a4 b4 7. Na2 e6 8. Bxc4 Nbd7 9. O-O Qb6 10. Qe2 Bb7 11. e4 Be7 12. Rd1 O-O 13. d5 cxd5 14. exd5 exd5 15. Qxe7 dxc4 16. Be3 Qc7 17. Nxb4 a5 18. Nc2 Nd5 19. Bf4 Qc8 20. Qg5 N7f6 21. Be5 Ra6 22. Ncd4 h6 23. Qg3 Ne7 24. Nh4 Nh5 25. Qa3 Ng6 26. Nxg6 Rxg6 27. f3 Re8 28. Bd6 Kh7 29. Kf2 Qd8 30. Nf5 Qg5 31. Ng3 Qh4 32. Qc5 Ree6 33. Qxh5 Qxh5 34. Nxh5 Rxd6 35. Rxd6 Rxd6 36. Rc1 Rd2+ 37. Kg3 Ba6 38. Nf4 g5 39. Nh3 Kg6 40. Nf2 f5 41. Nd1 f4+ 42. Kh3 Bc8+ 43. g4 h5 44. Rxc4 hxg4+ 45. fxg4 Bb7 {Source: CHESS, Sept 1944, p188  Play was of a very high order in the fourth round of the premier tournament at Blackpool yesterday.- Gurnhill who had been badly out of form, against Rhodes showed some of the incisive play which is known to be characteristic of him. He played a variation of the Slav defence against Rhodes's Queen’s Gambit, and developed bis pieces rather more functionally than Rhodes did. A series of quite difficult manoeuvres gave him an advantage which he pressed effectively and closed the game with a neat mating, combination. ' (Manchester Guardian, 21 July 1944)} 0-1

