1. d4 {... The last round of the premier tourna­ment of the Blackpool Chess Congress, which closed yesterday, provided some interesting studies in temperament. Wahltuch, whose highest expectation was the second prize, played hard, resolute chess against Hooper, the assured winner. In a queen's pawn opening Hooper, as Black, played bishop to bishop's fourth on the third move, a debatable tactic, and Wahltuch played to maintain a good control on both sides of the board and manoeuvred cleverly to that end. Even­tually careful play brought about a level position in which the problem was to seize the king's file. Hooper missed a rather clever way of achieving this, and having failed was in a slightly inferior position when he blundered, losing the exchange and the game. Nevertheless, Hooper wins the tourna­ment with 3½ points out of 5; Wahltuch wins second prize with 3 points. ... The effect of these results was to give Abrahams, Rhodes, and Gurnhill a score of 2½ points each. Mieses scores 1 point, which, of course, does him nothing like justice. In general the closeness of the results shows that the tournament was well con­tested. Although all the players were out of practice the fifteen games which they jointly produced included nine or ten which are worthy of any British championship or minor masters tournament. The winner, Hooper, was of a relatively unknown quality, but showed positional judgment of a high order. The tournament was too short to give the more combative players a chance of recovering lost ground. On the whole, an excellent week's chess. (Manchester Guardian, 22 July 1944)} 1-0

